tel 1.0

base spectacular
import soundness

info   two renderings of a value as text: `show` for the people using a program, `inspect` for its programmers
detail
    _Spectacular_ separates the two reasons a value becomes text. `show` renders it for a
    **person** — the form that belongs in a message, a log line or on a screen — through a
    `Showable` instance, and is a compile error for a type which has none, so a value that
    should never reach a user simply has no instance. `inspect` renders it for a
    **programmer** — an unambiguous, one-line, source-like form for debugging and test output —
    through an `Inspectable` instance, and always succeeds, deriving a structural rendering
    or, in the last resort, marking a borrowed one.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import affirmations.yesNoAffirmation

    case class Person(name: Text, age: Int)

    t"Simon".show                     // t"Simon"
    t"Simon".inspect                  // t"t\"Simon\""
    Person(t"Simon", 72).inspect      // t"Person(name:t\"Simon\" ╱ age:72)"
    t"adult: ${true}"                 // t"adult: yes"
    ```

    The same `Showable` instances drive `t"…"` interpolation, so a substituted value is shown,
    and a `Showable` is also `Communicable`, so it can be substituted into an `m"…"` message.

    A `Boolean` has no single obvious rendering, so showing one needs an `Affirmation` in
    scope, chosen by importing one of the `affirmations` package's givens by name: `import
    soundness.*` does not import givens, and there is deliberately no default.

    `Inspectable`'s renderings follow one notation across every Soundness type, so that two
    types never render alike: `[a, b]` for a `List`, `{a, b}` for a `Set`, `{k → v}` for a
    `Map`, `⟦k → v⟧` for a `Ledger`, `⟨ a b ⟩` for a `Sequence`, `Name(field:value ╱ …)` for a
    product, `｢value｣` and `○` for a present and an absent `Optional`, `t"…"` and `'x'` for text
    and characters, and superscript suffixes for sized numbers (`42ᵘ⁸`) and ordinals (`1ˢᵗ`).
    A rendering which was *not* native carries a marker: `“…”` for a `toString`, `⸢…⸣` for a
    borrowed `Showable` form and `⸤…⸥` for a borrowed `Encodable in Text` form, which
    `Inspectable.fallbacks` finds.

    The library has one source set, `spectacular.core`, which compiles for every platform.
    Everything is exported to the `soundness` umbrella, and the `affirmations` choice package
    merges with the umbrella's own.

refer spectacular.Showable
refer spectacular.Inspectable
refer spectacular.show
refer spectacular.inspect
refer spectacular.Affirmation

entry .show   renders a value as text for a person to read, through its `Showable` instance
    An extension on any value whose type has a `Showable`: `value.show` is
    `Showable.text(value)`. A type with no instance cannot be shown, and asking is a compile
    error rather than a silent fallback to `toString`, so the absence of an instance is how a
    type says it is not meant for display.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    43.show                   // t"43"
    List(1, 2, 3).show        // t"[1, 2, 3]"
    ```

    Interpolating a value into `t"…"` shows it in the same way. For a debugging form which
    always succeeds, use `inspect`.

entry .inspect   renders a value as unambiguous text for a programmer, through its `Inspectable`
    An extension on any value: `value.inspect` is `Inspectable.text(value)`, and since
    `Inspectable.derived` covers every type, it never fails to compile. The rendering is
    compact, on one line, resembles valid source where that is cheap, and shows every piece
    of state relevant at the value's *static* type.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    t"Hello\nworld".inspect            // t"t\"Hello\\nworld\""
    (5: Optional[Int]).inspect         // t"｢5｣"
    Map(1 -> 2, 3 -> 4).inspect        // t"{1 → 2, 3 → 4}"
    ```

    The rendering depends on the static type: a value typed `Any` falls back to `toString`,
    marked `“…”`. See `Inspectable` for the notation and the fallback chain.

entry .Showable   the typeclass of values which may be displayed to a person, as `value is Showable`
  detail
      A single-method typeclass, `text(value: Self): Text`, so an instance is written as a
      function from the value to its text. It extends `Communicable`, so a showable value can
      also be substituted into a `m"…"` message, as a `Message` of its text.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      case class Money(pence: Int)
      given Money is Showable = money => t"£${money.pence/100}.${money.pence%100}"

      Money(1099).show   // t"£10.99"
      ```

      `Showable` is deliberately demanding: there is no derivation and no fallback, so a type
      shows only if someone has decided how it should look. The companion holds instances for
      the primitive types, `Text`, `String`, `Message`, `Bytes`, every Scala `enum`, every
      `Textualizable` type, and the Soundness collections. A `Double` needs a
      `DecimalConverter` in scope and a `Boolean` an `Affirmation`, since neither has a single
      context-free form.

      An instance for a type which is refined (a branded list, a literal type) should be
      written over the subtypes of its subject (`[self <: Foo] => self is Showable`), since
      `Self` is invariant and an instance for `Foo` does not cover `Foo` with a refinement.
  refer spectacular.show
  refer spectacular.Inspectable
  refer spectacular.Affirmation
  entry #text   renders a value as text
  entry #message   renders a value as a `Message`, for `m"…"` interpolation
      `Message(text(value))`: the member which makes every `Showable` a `Communicable`.
  entry .showable   shows any `Textualizable` value through its textualization
  entry .text   shows `Text`, and any subtype of it, verbatim
  entry .string   shows a `String` as its text
  entry .char   shows a `Char` as a one-character text
  entry .long   shows a `Long` in decimal, with no suffix
  entry .int   shows an `Int` in decimal
  entry .short   shows a `Short` in decimal
  entry .byte   shows a `Byte` in decimal
  entry .message   shows a `Message` as its text
  entry .double   shows a `Double` through the `DecimalConverter` in scope
      Needs a `DecimalConverter`, which decides the number of digits, the notation and the
      rounding; without one, a `Double` does not show at all.
  entry .boolean   shows a `Boolean` through the `Affirmation` in scope
      Needs an `Affirmation`, imported by name from the `affirmations` package or defined
      locally; without one, a `Boolean` does not show at all, since presuming `true` and
      `false` would be presuming the context.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import affirmations.onOffAffirmation

      true.show   // t"on"
      ```
  entry .option   shows a Scala `Option`: its value, or `none`
  entry .bytes   shows a `Bytes` count through its own `text` rendering
  entry .enumeration   shows a case of any Scala `enum` by its name
      Every `reflect.Enum` shows as its `toString`, the case's name, so an enum needs no
      instance of its own unless a different form is wanted; a parameterised case shows with
      its arguments but without labels.
  entry .set   shows a `Set` as `{a, b}`, each element shown
  entry .list   shows a `List` as `[a, b]`, each element shown
      Written over the subtypes of `List[element]`, so a branded list (a `List` known to be
      populated) shows too.
  entry .sequence   shows a `Sequence` as `[ a b ]`, each element shown
  entry .none   shows `None` as `none`
  entry .specializable   shows a `Specializable` marker by its lowercased name
  entry .zerary   shows an `Ordinal` in its zero-based form, `3.₀`
  entry .typeRepr   shows a macro's `TypeRepr` as its source name, inside a macro
      Needs a `Quotes`; the name is rendered as stenography renders a type.
  entry .meta   shows a macro's `Type[T]` as its source name, inside a macro

entry .Inspectable   the typeclass of debug renderings, which every value has, as `value is Inspectable`
  detail
      A single-method typeclass, `text(value: Self): Text`, rendering a value for a
      programmer to read in a debugger or in test output. Unlike `Showable` it is total: when
      a type has no instance of its own, `derived` (inherited from `Inspectable2`) supplies
      one, trying in turn

      1. an `Encodable in Text`, borrowed and marked `⸤…⸥`;
      2. a `Showable`, borrowed and marked `⸢…⸣`;
      3. an `Optional` type (`Mandatable`), as `｢value｣` or `○`;
      4. a structural rendering through `Derivation`, for any case class, enum or sealed
          hierarchy;
      5. the value's `toString`, marked `“…”`.

      A native instance in the companion of the type, or here, takes precedence over all of
      them. The marks are deliberate: a borrowed rendering is a wire form or a human form, not
      a debug form, and a reader should see at a glance that a type lacks an instance.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      enum Shape:
        case Circle(radius: Int)
        case Point

      (Shape.Circle(5): Shape).inspect   // t"Circle(radius:5)"
      (t"Simon", 72).inspect             // t"(t\"Simon\" ╱ 72)"
      (name = t"Simon", age = 72).inspect   // t"(name:t\"Simon\" ╱ age:72)"
      ```

      Since a missing instance is never a compile error, coverage can regress silently: a
      type gains a refinement and its rendering drops to `toString`. `.fallbacks` is what a
      library's tests assert on to prevent that. An instance should be written over the
      subtypes of its subject (`[self <: Port] => self is Inspectable`), because `Self` is
      invariant and an instance for `Port` does not cover `Port over Tcp`; for a
      parameterised subject, bind the parameter with a wildcard (`[self <: Cookie[?]]`)
      rather than a second type parameter, which may silently fail to resolve. The opaque
      collections `List`, `Set`, `Map` and `Chain` are the exceptions, and keep an exact
      `Self`. An instance belongs in the subject's companion, not at top level, so that it
      needs no import to be found.

      The notation in use, by category:

      - containers: `[a, b]` list, `{a, b}` set, `{k → v}` map, `⟦k → v⟧` ledger, `⟨ a b ⟩`
        sequence, `⦋…⦌` array, `⁅…⁆` frozen array, `a ⋰ b ⋰ ..?` a lazy chain
      - products: `Name(field:value ╱ field2:value)`, `(a ╱ b)` tuple, `(name:value ╱ …)`
        named tuple
      - optionality: `｢value｣` present, `○` absent
      - text: `t"…"` with escapes, `'x'` a character
      - numbers: `3`, `3L`, `3.1F`, `3.toByte`, `BigInt(42)`; `42ᵘ⁸`, `-7ˢ³²`, `2Fᵇ⁸`,
        `3.14ᶠ⁶⁴` for the sized numbers
      - positions: `1ˢᵗ` ordinal, `1ˢᵗ‥5ᵗʰ` interval, `⟪4:8+5⟫` span
  refer spectacular.inspect
  refer spectacular.Inspectable2
  refer spectacular.Showable
  entry #text   renders a value for a programmer to read
  entry #contramap   an instance for another type, rendering it through a conversion to this one
      `inspectable.contramap[Celsius](_.degrees)` renders a `Celsius` as its degrees would
      render. The lambda must be pure.
  entry .fallbacks   those of some renderings which were not native: carrying `“…”`, `⸢…⸣` or `⸤…⸥`
      Returns the renderings, in order, which contain one of the three markers of a borrowed
      or `toString` rendering. A library's tests assert that its own types all inspect
      natively, and should pair that with a case asserting that an uncovered type *is*
      reported, so the check cannot pass vacuously.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      Inspectable.fallbacks(t"text".inspect, 'x'.inspect, Set(1, 2).inspect)   // Nil
      ```
  entry .marker   whether a character is one of the fallback markers `“`, `⸢` or `⸤`
  entry .escape   escapes a character as it would be written in a Scala literal
      Newlines, tabs, carriage returns, backslashes, quotes, apostrophes, backspaces and form
      feeds take their backslash escapes; any other character outside printable ASCII becomes
      `\uXXXX`. With the second parameter `true`, the escape character is written `\e`, as in a
      `t"…"` literal.
  entry .Derivation   the structural derivation of `Inspectable` for products and sums
    detail
        A wisteria `Derivable[Inspectable]`. A product renders as `Name(field:value ╱ …)`, with
        each field rendered by the instance for its static type, and a case object with no
        fields as its bare name; a tuple omits the name and labels. A sum renders the variant
        the value is. `derived` reaches it for any type with a `Reflection`, and `Inspectable.enumeration`
        for every Scala `enum`.
    entry .conjunction   the rendering of a product: its name and labelled fields
    entry .disjunction   the rendering of a sum: the rendering of its variant
  entry .char   renders a `Char` as a quoted, escaped literal, `'x'`
  entry .int   renders an `Int` in decimal
  entry .long   renders a `Long` with its `L` suffix, `3L`
  entry .byte   renders a `Byte` as `3.toByte`
  entry .short   renders a `Short` as `3.toShort`
  entry .string   renders a `String` as an escaped string literal, `"…"`
  entry .text   renders a `Text` as an escaped `t"…"` literal
      Every control character and quote is escaped as Scala would escape it, and the escape
      character as `\e`, so a text containing a newline is distinguishable from one containing `\` and `n`
      and the rendering could be pasted back into code.
  entry .float   renders a `Float` with its `F` suffix, and its infinities and NaN by name
  entry .double   renders a `Double`, and its infinities and NaN by name (`Double.NaN`)
  entry .boolean   renders a `Boolean` as `true` or `false`, with no `Affirmation` needed
  entry .unit   renders `()`
  entry .bigInt   renders a `BigInt` as `BigInt(42)`
  entry .bigDecimal   renders a `BigDecimal` as `BigDecimal(1.5)`
  entry .unset   renders `Unset` as `○`
  entry .reflectEnum   renders a value statically typed `reflect.Enum` by its `toString`
      Only for a value typed as `reflect.Enum` itself, for which no reflection is available; a
      value of a known enum type is rendered structurally by `.enumeration`.
  entry .enumeration   derives the rendering of any Scala `enum`, with field labels: `Circle(radius:5)`
      Takes priority over the borrowed `Showable` rendering every enum has (which would lose
      the labels), while yielding to an enum which defines an instance of its own.
  entry .tuple   renders a tuple as `(a ╱ b)`, each element by its static type's instance
      Inline, since it walks the element types known at the summon site. A value typed as a
      bare `Tuple`, whose elements are unknown, renders as its `toString`, marked `“…”`.
  entry .namedTuple   renders a named tuple as `(name:a ╱ age:b)`
      A named tuple is an opaque alias, which `Derivation` cannot see into, so it has this
      instance of its own. An element with no native instance degrades on its own.
  entry .u8   renders a `U8` with the suffix `ᵘ⁸`: `200ᵘ⁸`
  entry .u16   renders a `U16` with the suffix `ᵘ¹⁶`
  entry .u32   renders a `U32` with the suffix `ᵘ³²`
  entry .u64   renders a `U64` with the suffix `ᵘ⁶⁴`
  entry .s8   renders an `S8` with the suffix `ˢ⁸`
  entry .s16   renders an `S16` with the suffix `ˢ¹⁶`
  entry .s32   renders an `S32` with the suffix `ˢ³²`: `-7ˢ³²`
  entry .s64   renders an `S64` with the suffix `ˢ⁶⁴`
  entry .b8   renders a `B8` as two uppercase hexadecimal digits with the suffix `ᵇ⁸`: `2Fᵇ⁸`
  entry .b16   renders a `B16` as four zero-padded hexadecimal digits with the suffix `ᵇ¹⁶`
  entry .b32   renders a `B32` as eight zero-padded hexadecimal digits with the suffix `ᵇ³²`
  entry .b64   renders a `B64` as sixteen hexadecimal digits with the suffix `ᵇ⁶⁴`
  entry .f32   renders an `F32` with the suffix `ᶠ³²`, and its infinities as `∞` and `-∞`
  entry .f64   renders an `F64` with the suffix `ᶠ⁶⁴`: `3.5ᶠ⁶⁴`
  entry .q32   renders a `Q32` rational as a fraction with the suffix `ʳ³²`: `3/4ʳ³²`
  entry .q64   renders a `Q64` rational as a fraction with the suffix `ʳ⁶⁴`
  entry .bcd   renders a `Bcd` decimal with the suffix `ᵇᶜᵈ`
  entry .decimal   renders a `Decimal` with the suffix `ᵈ`: `3.14ᵈ`
  entry .ordinal   renders an `Ordinal` one-based, with its English suffix: `1ˢᵗ`, `3ʳᵈ`, `11ᵗʰ`
      The one-based number is the one a programmer counts with; the zero-based form is an
      implementation detail, and showing it would make every rendering off by one.
  entry .interval   renders an `Interval` as `1ˢᵗ‥5ᵗʰ`, or `∅` when empty
  entry .span   renders a `Span` by its shape: `⟪∅⟫`, `⟪@4+5⟫`, `⟪4:8+5⟫`, `⟪4‥8⟫` or `⟪4:8‥6:2⟫`
      A `Span` packs one of five ranges into a `Long`: empty, an offset and length, a line,
      column and length, a range of whole lines, or an area. The numbers are one-based.
  entry .bytes   renders a `Bytes` count exactly, as `4194304B`, never rounded
  entry .digit   renders a `Digit` with the suffix `ᵈᵍ`
  entry .message   renders a `Message` as `m"…"`
  entry .set   renders a `Set` as `{a, b}`
  entry .map   renders a `Map` as `{k → v, …}`, whose entries have no significant order
  entry .ledger   renders a `Ledger` as `⟦k → v, …⟧`, in insertion order
      Bracketed differently from a `Map` because a `Ledger`'s order is significant and a
      `Map`'s is not, and the rendering must say which a value is.
  entry .sequence   renders a `Sequence` as `⟨ 1 2 3 ⟩`
  entry .list   renders a `List` as `[a, b]`
  entry .array   renders a Scala array as `⦋…⦌`, with its element kind and subscripted indices
      The prefix names the JVM element type (`🅸` for `Int`, `🅻` for objects, …) and the
      dimension, and each element is preceded by its index in subscript digits.
  entry .arraySeq   renders a mutable `ArraySeq` as an array, closed with `⦌ₛ`
  entry .stream   renders a `Chain` lazily: `1 ⋰ 2 ⋰ 3 ⋰ ..?`, never forcing it
      Shows at most three forced elements; an unforced tail is `∿∿∿` and the end is `⯁`.
  entry .frozenArray   renders a frozen `Array[element]^{}` as `⁅…⁆`, with subscripted indices
  entry .option   renders a Scala `Option` as `Some(3)` or `None`
  entry .none   renders `None`

entry .Inspectable2   the lower-priority home of `Inspectable.derived`, the total fallback
  detail
      A trait which `Inspectable`'s companion extends, so that `derived` ranks below every
      instance written in the companion. Not used directly; its `derived` is found through
      the companion.
  entry #derived   the instance for any type without one: borrowed, derived, or `toString`
      Tries an `Encodable in Text` (marked `⸤…⸥`), a `Showable` (marked `⸢…⸣`), an `Optional`
      type (`｢value｣` or `○`), a `Reflection` (structural, through `Inspectable.Derivation`),
      and finally the value's `toString` (marked `“…”`). A type whose encoded form is escaped
      or encoded must define its own instance, since the borrowed form would mislead.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      given Exception is Showable = _ => t"<exception>"
      Exception("error message").inspect   // t"⸢<exception>⸣"
      ```

entry .Affirmation   the pair of words a `Boolean` shows as: `yes`/`no`, `on`/`off`, …
  detail
      A case class of two texts, chosen as a contextual value: `Showable.boolean` needs one,
      so a `Boolean` shows only once the context has said how. The `affirmations` package has
      four, each imported by name; any other pair is a one-line given.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      given Affirmation = Affirmation(t"+", t"-")
      t"${true}${false}"   // t"+-"
      ```
  refer spectacular.affirmations
  entry #yes   the text for `true`
  entry #no   the text for `false`
  entry #apply   the text for a boolean: `yes` if it is `true`, and `no` otherwise

entry .affirmations   the standard ways to show a `Boolean`, each a given to import by name
  detail
      Showing a `Boolean` needs an `Affirmation`, and none is in scope by default:
      `import soundness.*` brings none of these in, since `import pkg.*` does not import
      givens, and one is imported by name.

      ```scala fuel
      frame
        label Yes and no
        code
            import soundness.*
            import affirmations.yesNoAffirmation

            t"${true} ${false}"   // t"yes no"
      frame
        label On and off
        code
            import soundness.*
            import affirmations.onOffAffirmation

            t"${true} ${false}"   // t"on off"
      frame
        label True and false
        code
            import soundness.*
            import affirmations.trueFalseAffirmation

            t"${true} ${false}"   // t"true false"
      frame
        label One and zero
        code
            import soundness.*
            import affirmations.oneZeroAffirmation

            t"${true} ${false}"   // t"1 0"
      ##
      ```
  entry .yesNoAffirmation   shows `true` as `yes` and `false` as `no`
  entry .onOffAffirmation   shows `true` as `on` and `false` as `off`
  entry .trueFalseAffirmation   shows `true` as `true` and `false` as `false`
  entry .oneZeroAffirmation   shows `true` as `1` and `false` as `0`
